{
  "reasoning": "BFCL evaluation PASSED despite model output showing excessive tool chaining across all turns. The evaluation validates that final state (files created, copied, archived with correct content) matches ground truth, not the path taken to achieve it.",
  "error_identification": "Model chained multiple exploratory commands (pwd/ls/cd) with state mutations in single turns, and repeated cp/mv operations redundantly (Turn 3: 6 copy/move attempts; Turn 2: ls+diff+cat+cat chain). Per playbook deltas 10, 14, 19, 20, these should be atomic single operations per turn.",
  "root_cause_analysis": "Model prioritized exhaustive verification and retry logic over atomic execution. When initial cp calls appeared to fail or seemed incomplete, model looped with alternative paths (cd into Archived, relative paths, mv rename) rather than yielding after first attempt and awaiting next user turn.",
  "correct_approach": "Execute one atomic operation per turn: Turn 3 should be single `cp TeamNotes.txt Archived/IdeasArchive.txt` with immediate yield; Turn 4 should be single `cat IdeasArchive.txt`. Reserve exploratory ls/pwd for separate turns only if user requests verification.",
  "key_insight": "Evaluation passes on outcome correctness (state matches), not execution efficiency. However, playbook deltas 10, 14, 19, 20 explicitly forbid chaining; model should follow atomic discipline even when evaluation tolerates the redundancy. This scenario highlights gap between 'works' and 'follows protocol'—adhere strictly to single-operation-per-turn discipline."
}

